    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification required all **rejection responses (400, 409, 429)** to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nThe codebase already had the target infrastructure \u2014 a `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper and several handlers (`ValidationExceptionHandler`, `ForbiddenExceptionHandler`, `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`, `GeneralExceptionHandler`) using Spring's `ProblemDetail`. But nine rejection handlers still emitted a plain `Map<String, List<String>>` (`{\"errors\": [...]}`) with `application/json`. I converted each to the `ProblemDetail` pattern:\n\n**400 (Bad Request):** `MissingOwnerFields`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidTelephone`, `DisposableEmail`, `FutureRegistrationDate`\n**409 (Conflict):** `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n**429 (Too Many Requests):** `DailyOwnerLimit`\n\nEach now:\n- Responds via `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>>` (yielding the `application/problem+json` content type from `ProblemDetail`'s media type).\n- Builds the body through `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)`, which populates `type` (`about:blank`), `title`, `status`, and `detail` \u2014 plus the existing `timestamp`/`schemaValidationErrors` properties for consistency with the other handlers.\n- Preserves the original field information as an `errors` extension property, so no diagnostic detail was lost. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\nThe `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` acceptance test (missing `city` \u2192 400) now asserts `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`, and passes along with the full suite.",
